    "OtherAbstract": {
      "Type": "PIP",
      "Language": "eng",
      "AbstractText": "Ibuprofen, a prostaglandin synthestase inhibitor, was studied by double-blind technique to determine its value in relief of severe primary dysmenorrhea; 7 patients and a total of 23 menstrual cycles were studied. The amount of prostaglandin in the menstrual fluid of the 8 untreated cycles studied was higher than in nondysmenorrheic subjects. In the 7 ibuprofen-treated cycles, good to excellent relief of dysmenorrhea was recored which was associated with a 3- to 4-fold reduction in menstrual prostaglandin released. 5 placebo cycles revealed only poor or minimal relief of dysmenorrhea, and the menstrual prostaglandin released showed little difference from controls. There was a marked correlation between the severity of menstrual pain, as assessed daily by individual patients, and the level of menstrual prostaglandin released during the corresponding period. Results on menstrual fluid volume showed ibuprofen's effect to be inconsistent; however in some cycles a slight (10%) reduction in menstrual fluid volume was recorded."
    }
